**Verdict: APPROVE-WITH-NITS.** All seven checklist items of the binding rulings are implemented as ruled; the check order, refusal-as-data discipline, scope cuts, and fixture reshaping all verify against UCF 7.2.1/7.4.3/7.7.8/7.9 (as amended r5) and linker-dht 14.2.2–14.2.3. The three findings below are diagnostic/coverage nits; none blocks commit.

## Checklist verification

**(1) Rulings' items 1–7 — all present.**
- Segment address under the address's algorithm: `checkpoint.cljc:402-406` gates on `jing/segment-bytes-match?` (total, registry-parsed, `dao/jing.cljc:398-409`); the sha256 acceptance test is `checkpoint_test.cljc:205-209`.
- Hash-mismatch carries claimed and computed: `checkpoint.cljc:404-406`; `computed-address` (`checkpoint.cljc:379-387`) digests under the algorithm the address names, falling back to BLAKE3 for a non-segment/unregistered address (matches ruling 2; `parse-segment-address` returns nil for `:segment/md5-00`, so the fallback is unreachable-throw-free).
- Ops are BLAKE3 digests of canonical bytes of `[:yin.k/append target payload]`: `checkpoint.cljc:245-246, 348-350`; target/payload key choices per variant match ruling 3 and 7.4.3's required keys (`checkpoint.cljc:238-242`).
- Non-nil emitter: `checkpoint.cljc:164`; fixture `origin-nil-emitter` (`checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:274-275`), expected at `[:yin.k/origin]` (`checkpoint_test.cljc:148`).
- Every `:install` pending has its entry, at root and in children: `checkpoint.cljc:287-291` runs inside `pending-ops`, which `tree-ops` calls for the root and every nested child (`checkpoint.cljc:324, 335`); refusal is `:yin.k/undecodable` at the pending's path, kind `:incomplete-install`, matching 7.4.3's "a body carrying such a pending is `:yin.k/undecodable`". Fixture `install-without-entry` (`checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:290-291`).
- Fixture file: 38,261 bytes / 660 lines (verified with `wc`), 47 blocks, exactly the three anchors (`first-park`, `successor`, `installs`) carry hex; every non-anchor fixture is `[name base f]` over five accepted bases (`checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:241-310`); `non-canonical` patches the successor anchor at test time (`checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:342-348, 409`).

**(2) Order, data-only refusals, no engine, handoff untouched.** Order is bytes/hash → canonical decode → tag → version gate → kind → header/child role → ids with recursion (`checkpoint.cljc:399-411, 364-376`), matching 7.9's "before every other check but the bytes and the tag". `answer` (`checkpoint.cljc:88-95`) converts only vocabulary-carrying throws to data; I audited every helper call for raw-throw paths — `numeric-kind` is always guarded by `numeric?`, `content=`/`content-key` cannot throw on decoded values (decode returns safe-or-bigint integers, `cbor.cljc:223-232`, so the cljs unsafe-integer refusal at `cbor.cljc:865-869` is unreachable), and decode failures are caught totally at `checkpoint.cljc:407-410`. Requires only `dao.jing` and `dao.jing.cbor` (`checkpoint.cljc:42-43`). `git status --porcelain` shows only the four new files plus collab docs; `handoff.cljc` is untouched.

**(3) Version gate and op-id rules.** The gate checks `integer-kind?` (`checkpoint.cljc:102-104, 129`), so `1.0` refuses on every host (fixture `version-float` uses the `float64` carrier; the cljs carrier keeps the kind). Root v0 → `:profile-mismatch` (7.2.1 exclusive-only); child v0 under v1 → `:undecodable` mixed-version with path, unpublished child versions → `:profile-mismatch` with path — exactly ruling 8. Op-ids: exactly two keys (`checkpoint.cljc:254`), seq exact and below the root counter strictly (`counter-at-bound` pins inclusivity at 2^52−1 and its last id at 2^52−2), child ids checked in the root's ctx (occurrence/origin/counter passed down, `checkpoint.cljc:373-376, 335`), op-id only on the three retaining variants, global duplicate detection in canonical traversal order (root pendings, then children depth-first in `encoded-compare` name order). Ancestry itself is not checked — C9's, per ruling 11.

**(4) Loader, file, test quality.** `read-file` uses `fx/read-path`, which reads repo-relative on all three hosts (`test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41`); the render-equality test recomputes the whole file per host, pinning codec/host agreement. D reuses the file as names + pinned addresses + three frozen byte anchors; the fixtures docstring states the restorable-machine caveat and the REPL regeneration path. Mutation quality: the engineer's red/green evidence (stub → 120 failures; the two amendment fixtures verified accepted-before/refused-after) is credible, and I confirmed each refusal class has a discriminating fixture (e.g. removing the variant check would crash `intent`'s `case` rather than pass; a `<=` bound would accept `op-id-at-counter`; a BLAKE3-only verifier fails the sha256 test).

**(5) D reuse and scope.** Nothing checks code hashes, cells, registers, parked-id, result, frames-nonempty, halted-forbids-frames, or install `:phase`/`:parent` — grep-verified; exactly the deferred list. The one shape the inspector does impose beyond the header — the stream marker's `:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream` (`checkpoint.cljc:228`) — matches the real marker the engine mints (`engine.cljc:671-673`), so D's export cannot drift from it. `:yin.k/kind` on the baseline is the accepted deviation 1.

**(6) Docstring.** `checkpoint.cljc:36-40` states the carried intent serves variant comparison and id membership only and is never a dedup comparand, with the reason.

## Findings

1. `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/checkpoint.cljc:202-203` (and `:258-259`) — **nit (diagnostics)** — the `:inexact` kind is used both for a wrong-kind value (`counter-float`, `op-id-seq-float`) and for an out-of-range one (`counter-over-bound`, `counter-negative`), and the refusal carries no found value, so a caller cannot distinguish the two cases 7.7.8 states separately ("of another kind" / "outside the range"). Fix (optional, before D locks onto kinds): carry the found value, or split the kind (`:inexact` vs `:out-of-range`). Tests at `checkpoint_test.cljc:149-151, 172-173` pin the current behavior and would follow.
2. `test/yin/vm/ucf/checkpoint_test.cljc:205-218` — **nit (coverage)** — `:yin.k/computed` is proven under the named algorithm only in the matching direction (sha256 accept) and in the BLAKE3 fallback direction (four non-segment cases); no test shows a mismatch under a *named non-default* algorithm (e.g. a sha256 address over the successor's bytes should compute a `:segment/sha256-…` address, not a BLAKE3 one). Fix: add one doseq case to `bytes-must-hash-to-the-address-and-be-canonical`.
3. `test/yin/vm/ucf/checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:290-291` — **nit (coverage)** — the entry-for-every-install rule is fixture-exercised at the root only; a child's `:install` pending without an entry travels the identical `pending-ops` path (`checkpoint.cljc:287-291` via `tree-ops`), but nothing pins it. The ruling asked for "a fixture" and one exists, so this is optional; if added, mutate the `'bar` entry out of the nested `child` body and expect `:incomplete-install` at `[:yin.k/installs foo :yin.k/child :yin.k/frames 0 :yin.k/pending]`.

Nothing else rose to a finding: the `non-canonical` patch's `replace-first` anchors on the top-level `:yin.k/version` (the nested contract copy encodes `00`, not `01`) and is pinned anyway by its address and refusal class; the halted-root id handling (`:op-seq-range` for any id, no counter) is the accepted ruling-10 outcome; `version-absent`'s explicit nil in the refusal map matches 7.9's "as found". The file is data D can consume as-is, with the documented one-commit address re-pin when D regenerates the bases through a real export.
